            <ArticleTitle>Pain in multiple sclerosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1270-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We reviewed 317 patients with multiple sclerosis (MS) and found that the incidence of clinically significant pain, excluding headache and paresthesia, was 28.8%. Successful treatment requires recognition of the pathophysiology of the pain syndromes encountered in MS. Antidepressant drugs have been of particular value in the treatment of chronic pain in these patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
